# How you actually link papers to code

There are several evidence strengths.

```text
VERY STRONG
paper explicitly links repo
repo CITATION/README explicitly links paper
same author/lab owns repo

STRONG
repo says "implementation of <paper>"
paper DOI/arXiv ID in source/docs
commit message explicitly references paper

MEDIUM
matching novel terminology
matching algorithm/config names
same benchmark + method
release shortly after paper
author identity overlap uncertain

WEAK
embedding / abstract similarity
same general research topic
```

Then calculate something like:

```text
implementation_confidence =
    explicit_reference
  + authorship_overlap
  + method_signature_match
  + benchmark_overlap
  + temporal_plausibility
  + code_structure_evidence
  - contradiction_penalties
```

And retain **every reason** rather than emitting an opaque score.

This matters because static paper↔repo linking already exists in resources like Papers With Code, which explicitly models papers, code, datasets, methods and evaluation tables. ([Papers with Code][1]) The harder and more interesting problem is reliable traceability: prior empirical work found that repository↔paper links are incomplete and often one-directional, with many papers failing to link back to implementations. ([Papers with Code][2])

Your version should therefore be **temporal + probabilistic + evidence-backed**, rather than another Papers With Code clone.

